  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to use a particle swarm optimization (PSO) algorithm to search for the optimal step function h(x) that minimizes the maximum overlap integral. This approach addresses the limitations of prior methods by leveraging PSO's population-based exploration to avoid local minima, especially when combined with a structured initial population derived from the initial_h_values. The strategy focuses on maintaining global diversity in the search space while applying constraint-preserving updates to ensure all candidates remain feasible.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nRepresentation: A step function h(x) over [0, 2] with n_points samples, discretized at dx = 2.0 / n_points. h[i] \u2208 [0, 1], \u2211h[i] = n_points/2. \nObjective: Minimize C\u2085 = max_k [\u222b h(x)(1 - h(x + k)) dx], equivalent to minimizing max_k [1 - \u222b h(x)h(x + k) dx], or maximizing min_k [\u222b h(x)h(x + k) dx]. \nConstraints: \n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n- \u2211h[i] = n_points/2\nEvaluation: Use np.correlate(h, 1 - h, mode=\"full\") * dx to compute all lags and take the maximum value.\nSurrogate: Direct evaluation of the original objective function. PSO is used to search the space while respecting constraints. \n\n3. Implementation procedure\nInput: n_points (e.g., 200), seed, budget_s.\nParent validation: If initial_h_values is provided, use it as the seed for the swarm. If not, generate a random initial configuration with n_points/2 ones spread evenly.\nInitialization:\n- Generate a population of particles, each as a vector of size n_points.\n- Start with the initial_h_values scaled to meet the sum constraint.\n- For other particles, generate random samples with exactly n_points/2 ones, spread across the interval.\n\nCore update loop:\n1. For each particle:\n   a. Compute current fitness (C\u2085 value).\n   b. Update velocity and position using PSO rules:\n      - v = \u03c9*v + c1*r1*(pBest - x) + c2*r2*(gBest - x)\n      - x = x + v\n2. Clip values to [0, 1] and normalize to \u2211h = n_points/2:\n   - h_clipped = np.clip(x, 0.0, 1.0)\n   - h_normalized = h_clipped * (target_sum / np.sum(h_clipped))\n3. Evaluate fitness of updated h_normalized.\n4. Update personal best (pBest) and global best (gBest) for each particle.\n\nTermination:\n- Stop when time budget is exhausted.\n- Return the global best particle as the solution.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nDefault configuration:\n- n_points = 200\n- Particle count = 50\n- Inertia weight \u03c9 = 0.7\n- Cognitive coefficient c1 = 1.5\n- Social coefficient c2 = 1.5\n- Learning rates adjusted for large n_points.\n\nVariations:\n- Adaptive inertia weight decay (initial 0.7, decay to 0.5)\n- Different initial population generation (sine wave patterns, binary Golay sequences)\n- Hybrid with SLSQP refinement for final iterations\n- Use initial_h_values as part of the initial swarm for warm start\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nTime estimation:\n- PSO is O(pop_size * iterations * n_points), with approximate 100 iterations for 600s budget.\n- Memory: ~200KB for swarm (50 particles * 200 values)\n\nDeadline management:\n- Check time after every 10 iterations\n- If budget exhausted, return best solution with L-BFGS-B refinement\n\nRobustness:\n- Exact constraint satisfaction via clipping and normalization\n- Fallback to initial_h_values if no improvement for 20 iterations\n- Handle numerical instability via stochastic perturbation (\u00b10.05) to best candidate every 100 iterations\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFeasibility checks:\n- All h[i] \u2208 [0,1] via clipping\n- \u2211h = n_points/2 via normalization\n\nObjective consistency:\n- Re-evaluate correlation using original metric\n- Compare against fitness function output\n\nSanity checks:\n- Validate h has exactly n_points/2 ones after normalization\n- Confirm C\u2085 matches evaluation logic\n\nReturn format:\n- h_values.tolist() of shape (n_points,)\n- computed C\u2085 (float)\n- n_points (int)",
